\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces The quadric law \eqref  {eq:quadric}. A stationarity datum confines every vector of its design to one central quadric of the multiplier, an ellipsoid whenever $\Lambda $ is nonsingular. Drawn at $(6,3)$: the six vectors of a design, all on the ellipsoid of $\Lambda =\operatorname  {diag}(\tfrac  12,\tfrac  13,\tfrac  16)$ at $\nu =1$, with its three principal sections and its silhouette. By \eqref  {eq:multiplier} the surface is assembled from the tight directions, the semi-axis along $u_i$ having length $\sqrt  {\nu /\lambda _i}$: it is longest where the multiplier mass is least. Inset: at the tetrahedron of Example\nonbreakingspace \ref {ex:tetrakkt} the multiplier is $\tfrac  13I_3$ and the quadric is the sphere $\lVert x\rVert ^2=3$.}}{61}{figure.19}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:quadric}{{19}{61}{The quadric law \eqref {eq:quadric}. A stationarity datum confines every vector of its design to one central quadric of the multiplier, an ellipsoid whenever $\Lambda $ is nonsingular. Drawn at $(6,3)$: the six vectors of a design, all on the ellipsoid of $\Lambda =\diag (\tfrac 12,\tfrac 13,\tfrac 16)$ at $\cv =1$, with its three principal sections and its silhouette. By \eqref {eq:multiplier} the surface is assembled from the tight directions, the semi-axis along $u_i$ having length $\sqrt {\cv /\lambda _i}$: it is longest where the multiplier mass is least. Inset: at the tetrahedron of Example~\ref {ex:tetrakkt} the multiplier is $\tfrac 13I_3$ and the quadric is the sphere $\lVert x\rVert ^2=3$}{figure.19}{}}
